{"scorer":"scoring_2","scores":[{"idea_id":"bootstrap_code_stability","scores":{"novelty":3,"scientific_motivation":4,"feasibility":5,"downstream_impact":3,"experimental_distinctness":4},"rationale":"Explicitly exposing item-level quantization stability as a compact SID feature is a plausible, lightweight way to let the generator distinguish reliable from borderline codes. The idea is motivated by brittleness and is straightforward to test without changing the tokenizer or recommender objective.","uncertainties":"Gaussian perturbations based on embedding-coordinate variance may not represent realistic metadata uncertainty; stability may mostly reflect codebook geometry, and the generator may ignore or misuse the extra token. Magnitude and robustness of ranking gains are unknown."},{"idea_id":"train_only_demand_signature","scores":{"novelty":3,"scientific_motivation":4,"feasibility":5,"downstream_impact":4,"experimental_distinctness":4},"rationale":"A compact, train-only descriptor of repeat propensity and per-user demand shape addresses a real limitation of content-only identifiers: content similarity need not imply similar consumption behavior. It is cheap to implement and preserves the existing content SID, while the frequency-matched evaluation gives a meaningful test of information beyond raw popularity.","uncertainties":"Repeat statistics can be noisy for sparse items and may encode popularity or dataset-specific behavior rather than transferable demand regimes. It is unclear how much information remains after conditioning on frequency and sequence context, and whether the signature generalizes to cold items or shifting demand."},{"idea_id":"train_graph_denoised_residual","scores":{"novelty":3,"scientific_motivation":4,"feasibility":4,"downstream_impact":4,"experimental_distinctness":4},"rationale":"Using train-only transitions to make a bounded, popularity-corrected adjustment to item representations is a coherent way to reconcile content semantics with observed sequential substitutability before applying an unchanged tokenizer. Sparse graph aggregation and a small lambda sweep are practical, and the mechanism is distinct from transition-loss or graph-embedding approaches.","uncertainties":"Transition neighbors may capture navigation or co-occurrence rather than semantic substitutes, and hubs or sparse histories can distort the aggregate despite correction. The proposed similarity check does not establish improved semantic quality; effects on recommendation quality and long-tail items remain empirical."},{"idea_id":"bootstrap_consensus_sid","scores":{"novelty":4,"scientific_motivation":4,"feasibility":3,"downstream_impact":3,"experimental_distinctness":5},"rationale":"The proposal directly addresses tokenizer estimation variability through resampled replicas and aligned assignment consensus, which is conceptually distinct from input-uncertainty modeling or adding behavioral features. Soft consensus targets provide an implementable regularization mechanism and testable stability objective.","uncertainties":"Replica labels may not admit reliable one-to-one alignment when codebooks split or merge, particularly across residual quantization levels. Six tokenizer fits are a substantial cost, and lower assignment disagreement may not improve recommendation ranking or could reduce code utilization."},{"idea_id":"uncertainty_aware_sid","scores":{"novelty":4,"scientific_motivation":4,"feasibility":3,"downstream_impact":4,"experimental_distinctness":4},"rationale":"Training the tokenizer with multiple stochastic metadata views and an explicit code-agreement penalty targets stability at representation-learning time while keeping inference-time SID format unchanged. This is a principled extension of uncertainty-aware quantization, with a specific high-uncertainty subgroup hypothesis and recommendation-level evaluation.","uncertainties":"Metadata dropout may not approximate actual ambiguity or corruption, and the variance-weighted penalty could either over-regularize meaningful distinctions or underweight noisy views depending on scale. Multi-view encoder computation adds cost; whether improved code stability translates to held-out recommendation gains is unknown."}]}
